insert into public.social_posts (row_id,post_date,publish_at,content_type,ig_media_type,topic,asset_name,media_urls,caption,alt_text,approved,status,notes) values
('TFW-20261010-01','2026-10-10',('2026-10-30 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Static','IMAGE',$c$2026-10-10_Static_Phrases-to-Gain-Time$c$,'2026-10-10_Static_Phrases-to-Gain-Time',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-10/2026-10-10_Static_Phrases-to-Gain-Time_01.jpg'],$c$You know the answer, but the French word won't come. Most learners switch to English right there. Here's what to say instead.

These phrases give you two or three seconds to think and keep you speaking French. They work with a coworker, at the clinic, on a phone interview, and in the speaking part of TEF or TCF Canada.

10 phrases to gain time:
→ Comment dire… (How can I put it…)
→ C'est une bonne question. (That's a good question.)
→ Laissez-moi réfléchir. (Let me think.) With a friend: Laisse-moi réfléchir.
→ Je ne trouve pas le mot, mais… (I can't find the word, but…)
→ Autrement dit, … (In other words, …)
→ Voyons voir… (Let's see…)
→ Si je comprends bien, … (If I understand correctly, …)
→ C'est-à-dire que… (What I mean is…)
→ Je dirais que… (I'd say that…)
→ Euh… (Um…) Short and natural, as long as French follows.

Takeaway: pick two of these and use them every time you hesitate, until they come out on their own.

Want to practice them out loud with a teacher who corrects you? Book a trial class through the link in bio.

