('TFW-20261014-01','2026-10-14',('2026-11-02 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English$c$,'2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-14/2026-10-14_Carousel_Stop-Translating-From-English_06.jpg'],$c$« Je suis 30 ans. » « J'attends pour l'autobus. » They sound right because they're English, word for word. In French, they're mistakes.

When you build a sentence in English and swap the words, three things go wrong: you use être where French uses avoir, you add prepositions French doesn't need, and you copy expressions that don't exist. Swipe for 15 fixes.

10 of them:
→ Je suis 30 ans → J'ai 30 ans. (I'm 30.)
→ Je suis faim → J'ai faim. (I'm hungry.)
→ Je suis froid → J'ai froid. (I'm cold.) « Je suis froid » means you're a cold person.
→ J'attends pour l'autobus → J'attends l'autobus. (I'm waiting for the bus.)
→ Je cherche pour un emploi → Je cherche un emploi. (I'm looking for a job.)
→ Ça dépend sur l'horaire → Ça dépend de l'horaire. (It depends on the schedule.)
→ Je suis intéressé dans ce poste → Je suis intéressé par ce poste. (I'm interested in this job.)
→ J'applique à ce poste → Je postule à ce poste. (I'm applying for this job.) « Appliquer » is heard in informal Québec speech.
→ J'ai manqué toi → Tu me manques. (I miss you.)
→ Je suis confortable → Je suis à l'aise. (I'm comfortable.) « Confortable » is for things, like a chair.

Takeaway: when a sentence feels too easy to translate, check the verb (être or avoir?) and the little word after it (pour, sur, dans?). That's where most of these hide.

Quiz answers (slide 5): 1. J'ai 28 ans · 2. Ça dépend de mon horaire · 3. J'attends le métro

Reading the fix is step one. Losing the habit takes speaking practice with someone who stops you when it happens. Our classes have 1 to 3 students, so the teacher hears and corrects every sentence. Details and the trial class are in the bio.

