('TFW-20261015-01','2026-10-15',('2026-11-03 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy$c$,'2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_06.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-15/2026-10-15_Carousel_French-at-the-Pharmacy_07.jpg'],$c$Sore throat, fever, stuffy nose… could you explain it to a pharmacist in French? Most learners know « malade » and then freeze.

At a Canadian pharmacy you need three things: say what's wrong, say since when, and ask the right question. Swipe for the phrases, plus the Canadian words you won't find in a Paris textbook.

10 phrases to save:
→ J'ai mal à la gorge. (I have a sore throat.)
→ J'ai mal à la tête. (I have a headache.)
→ J'ai le nez bouché. (My nose is stuffy.)
→ Ça fait trois jours que je tousse. (I've been coughing for three days.)
→ Je fais de la fièvre depuis hier. (I've had a fever since yesterday.) Very common in Canada; « j'ai de la fièvre » works too.
→ Qu'est-ce que vous me conseillez ? (What do you recommend?)
→ Est-ce que je peux l'avoir sans ordonnance ? (Can I get it without a prescription?)
→ Est-ce que ça rend somnolent ? (Does it make you drowsy?)
→ Avez-vous de l'acétaminophène ? (Do you have acetaminophen?) In France: paracétamol.
→ une clinique sans rendez-vous (a walk-in clinic)

Takeaway: if it's still going on, use the present tense with depuis or ça fait… que. « Je tousse depuis hier », not « j'ai toussé depuis hier ».

Quiz answers (slide 6): 1. à la · 2. que · 3. l'acétaminophène

Want 3 full conversations (pharmacy, walk-in clinic, calling your manager) and 10 practice questions with answers? DM « PR » and I'll send you the practical guide.

