('TFW-20261016-01','2026-10-16',('2026-11-04 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend$c$,'2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_06.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_07.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-16/2026-10-16_Carousel_TEF-Section-B-Convince-a-Friend_08.jpg'],$c$Your friend says « Bof… » and you have about 10 minutes to change their mind. In French. That's TEF Canada oral, Section B.

You read an ad, present it to a friend (played by the examiner) and answer every objection. Many candidates list two facts and run out of things to say. What helps is a structure: present, argue, answer objections, close. Swipe for all 4 steps.

10 phrases to save:
→ J'ai vu une annonce qui va t'intéresser. (I saw an ad you'll be interested in.)
→ Tu m'as dit que tu voulais bouger plus, non ? (You told me you wanted to be more active, right?)
→ D'abord, c'est excellent pour la santé. (First, it's excellent for your health.)
→ En plus, on rencontre des gens sympas. (Plus, you meet nice people.)
→ Enfin, c'est vraiment abordable. (Finally, it's really affordable.)
→ Je comprends, mais tu peux commencer doucement. (I understand, but you can start slowly.)
→ C'est vrai. Par contre, ton après-midi est libre. (True. On the other hand, your afternoon is free.)
→ Ne t'inquiète pas, tous les niveaux sont acceptés. (Don't worry, all levels are welcome.)
→ On pourrait essayer une fois, juste pour voir. (We could try once, just to see.)
→ Alors, qu'est-ce que tu en penses ? (So, what do you think?)

Takeaway: it's a friend, so use tu from start to finish. And never answer an objection with « Non, tu as tort ». Agree first (je comprends, tu as raison), then turn it around with mais or par contre. The same habit helps in TCF Canada speaking when you defend an opinion.

Quiz answers (slide 7): 1. D'abord · 2. mais · 3. pourrait

Comment your 3 answers below and I'll correct every one.

