insert into public.social_posts (row_id,post_date,publish_at,content_type,ig_media_type,topic,asset_name,media_urls,caption,alt_text,approved,status,notes) values
('TFW-20261017-01','2026-10-17',('2026-11-05 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Static','IMAGE',$c$2026-10-17_Static_Narrate-Your-Day-in-French$c$,'2026-10-17_Static_Narrate-Your-Day-in-French',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-17/2026-10-17_Static_Narrate-Your-Day-in-French_01.jpg'],$c$Nobody to practice French with? There's one person who's with you all day: you.

Here's a study tip that takes five minutes. Say what you're doing, in French, while you're doing it. Making coffee, putting on your coat, waiting for the metro. Out loud when you're alone, in a whisper on the bus.

Why it helps: reading trains you to recognize French. Speaking trains you to produce it, and that's the part that goes blank in a real conversation or in the TEF/TCF Canada speaking test.

10 sentences to narrate your day:
→ Je me lève et je prépare mon café. (I get up and make my coffee.)
→ Je mets mon manteau : il fait froid ! (I put on my coat: it's cold!)
→ Je prends le métro jusqu'au centre-ville. (I take the metro downtown.)
→ Je lis mes courriels dans l'autobus. (I read my emails on the bus.)
→ Je commence le travail à 9 h. (I start work at 9.)
→ Je mange avec mes collègues à midi. (I eat with my coworkers at noon.)
→ Après le travail, je fais l'épicerie. (After work, I do the grocery shopping.)
→ Je prépare le souper. (I make supper.)
→ Ce soir, je vais appeler ma famille. (Tonight I'm going to call my family.)
→ Demain, je vais me lever plus tôt. (Tomorrow I'm going to get up earlier.)

In Canada you'll hear « faire l'épicerie » (France: faire les courses) and « le souper » for the evening meal (France: le dîner).

Takeaway: present tense for what you're doing now, « je vais + infinitive » for what's next. Tonight, narrate five things before bed.

Want the full 30-day speaking plan, with 3 real conversations from Canadian daily life and exercises with answers? DM me « PR » and I'll send it.

