('TFW-20261019-01','2026-10-19',('2026-11-07 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Static','IMAGE',$c$2026-10-19_Static_COD-Pronouns-Le-La-Les$c$,'2026-10-19_Static_COD-Pronouns-Le-La-Les',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-19/2026-10-19_Static_COD-Pronouns-Le-La-Les_01.jpg'],$c$Le formulaire ? « Oui, j'ai rempli le formulaire. » Correct… but nobody talks like that. A francophone would say: « Oui, je l'ai rempli. »

That little word is a direct object pronoun: le, la, les. It replaces a thing or a person you've already mentioned. The tricky part for English speakers: in French it goes BEFORE the verb.

le = masculine singular · la = feminine singular · les = plural · l' before a vowel

10 examples:
→ Le formulaire ? Je le remplis ce soir. (The form? I'm filling it out tonight.)
→ Ma carte de débit ? Je la cherche partout ! (My debit card? I'm looking for it everywhere!)
→ Mes courriels ? Je les lis le matin. (My emails? I read them in the morning.)
→ Le métro ? Je le prends tous les jours. (The metro? I take it every day.)
→ Ta gestionnaire ? Je l'appelle demain. (Your manager? I'm calling her tomorrow.)
→ L'autobus ? Je l'attends depuis 20 minutes. (The bus? I've been waiting for it for 20 minutes.)
→ Mes clés ? Je ne les trouve pas. (My keys? I can't find them.)
→ Le rapport ? Je vais le finir demain. (The report? I'm going to finish it tomorrow.)
→ La lettre ? Je l'ai envoyée hier. (The letter? I sent it yesterday.)
→ Le formulaire ? Remplissez-le en ligne. (The form? Fill it out online.)

Watch out: parler à, téléphoner à and répondre à use lui / leur, not le / la. « Je lui téléphone. »

Takeaway: before you say the noun a second time, swap it for le, la or les and put it in front of the verb (in front of the infinitive when there are two verbs).

Want 3 real conversations (Service Canada, the office, your landlord) and 10 exercises with answers? DM « PR » and I'll send you the practical guide.

