('TFW-20261020-01','2026-10-20',('2026-11-08 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters$c$,'2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_06.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_07.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-20/2026-10-20_Carousel_Silent-Final-Letters_08.jpg'],$c$« Un petiT café » ? If you pronounce every letter you see, your French will sound off, even when your grammar is perfect.

French writes a lot of letters it never says. The good news: a few rules cover most everyday words. Swipe for 4 rules plus the liaison bonus.

10 examples to read out loud:
→ un petit café (a small coffee): the t is silent
→ J'ai un rendez-vous. (I have an appointment.): the s is silent
→ Il fait trop froid ! (It's too cold!): p and d are silent
→ Bonjour ! (Hello!): the r is said
→ avec du lait (with milk): the c is said
→ un œuf (an egg): the f is said
→ le souper (supper): -er sounds like « é », the r is silent
→ Ils parlent français. (They speak French.): -ent is silent, it sounds like « il parle »
→ petit → petite (small, m. → f.): the t comes back
→ les amis (the friends): « lé-z-ami », that's a liaison

Takeaway: final d, p, s, t, x, z are usually silent. Final C, R, F, L (think CaReFuL) are usually said, except in -er words. Read the examples out loud once, then cover the slides and say them from memory.

Quiz answers (slide 7): 1. Said (sac) · 2. Silent (étudient) · 3. Silent (chaud)

These rules come from our Alphabet, Accents & Pronunciation guide, the one we use in class.

Said or silent? Comment your 3 answers below and I'll reply to every one.

