insert into public.social_posts (row_id,post_date,publish_at,content_type,ig_media_type,topic,asset_name,media_urls,caption,alt_text,approved,status,notes) values
('TFW-20261027-01','2026-10-27',('2026-11-15 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels$c$,'2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_06.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_07.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-27/2026-10-27_Carousel_French-Nasal-Vowels_08.jpg'],$c$Vent, vont, vin: the wind, they go, the wine. Three different words, and the only thing that changes is a nasal sound.

French has three main nasal vowels. The air goes through your nose and you don't pronounce the n or m at the end. Swipe for the spellings behind each sound, plus the one trap that cancels it.

10 examples to read out loud:
→ Je prends l'autobus. (I take the bus.): en = « an »
→ J'ai trente ans. (I'm thirty.): en and an, same sound
→ Je suis en retard. (I'm late.): en
→ Bonjour, ça va ? (Hello, how are you?): on
→ Ils sont à Montréal. (They're in Montréal.): on
→ Votre nom, s'il vous plaît ? (Your name, please?): om = « on »
→ Le train part à 8 h. (The train leaves at 8.): ain = « in »
→ J'ai faim ! (I'm hungry!): aim = « in »
→ Je travaille lundi. (I work on Monday.): un (in Québec it often keeps a rounder sound)
→ bon → Bonne journée ! (good → Have a good day!): nn + e, you hear the n, no nasal

Takeaway: an / am / en / em → « an » · on / om → « on » · in / im / ain / aim / ein / un → « in ». When a vowel or a second n / m follows, the nasal usually disappears and you hear a clear n.

Quiz answers (slide 7): 1. Not nasal (bonne) · 2. Nasal (ans) · 3. Not nasal (semaine)

These sounds come from our Alphabet, Accents & Pronunciation guide, the one we use in class to practice them out loud.

Nasal or not? Comment your 3 answers below and I'll reply to every one.

#LearnFrench #FrenchPronunciation #FrenchForPR #TEFCanada #TCFCanada #CanadaPR #ApprendreLeFrancais #FrenchA1 #FrenchInCanada #FrenchSounds$c$,$c$Navy brand carousel on the three French nasal vowels (an/en, on, in/un), the trap that cancels them and a nasal-or-not quiz.$c$,true,'READY','Backlog re-post'),
