('TFW-20261028-01','2026-10-28',('2026-11-16 20:00'::timestamp at time zone 'America/Toronto'),'Carousel','CAROUSEL',$c$2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De$c$,'2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De',array['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_01.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_02.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_03.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_04.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_05.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_06.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_07.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/thefrenchway2pr-ux/tfw2pr-media/main/media/2026-10-28/2026-10-28_Carousel_Article-Mistakes-Le-Du-De_08.jpg'],$c$« J'aime café. » « Je n'ai pas une voiture. » « Je suis une infirmière. » Three sentences that sound fine if you think in English. In French, all three need a fix.

Little words like le, du and de don't work the same way in English, so they're the first thing learners drop or mix up. Swipe for 25 fixes, grouped into 5 simple rules.

10 examples to read out loud:
→ J'aime le café. (I like coffee.) Likes take le / la / les.
→ Je déteste l'hiver. (I hate winter.)
→ Je bois du café le matin. (I drink coffee in the morning.) Some = du.
→ On mange de la poutine ce soir. (We're eating poutine tonight.)
→ Je voudrais de l'eau. (I'd like some water.)
→ Je n'ai pas de voiture. (I don't have a car.) After ne… pas: de.
→ On n'a pas de lait. (We don't have any milk.)
→ J'ai beaucoup d'amis ici. (I have a lot of friends here.) Quantity + de.
→ Une tasse de thé, s'il vous plaît. (A cup of tea, please.)
→ Je suis infirmière. (I'm a nurse.) No article for your job after être.

Watch out: aimer and être keep their article in the negative. Je n'aime pas le café. Ce n'est pas un problème.

Takeaway: before you speak, ask yourself: do I mean the thing in general (le), some of it (du), none of it (de), or my job (no article)?

Quiz answers (slide 7): 1. Je ne mange pas de gluten · 2. J'adore le sirop d'érable · 3. Je suis infirmier

Knowing the rule is the easy part. Using it while you speak takes practice with a teacher who catches it every time. Our classes have 1 to 3 students, so you get real feedback on every sentence. The trial class is in the bio.

